{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1825 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "ee486af10d4db4ae5e437fbca5f47f2c4355bb5b3be458fe82e14b930cfa29b9" :edb/db-before-t 1824 :edb/db-after-t 1825 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535137 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:48:26.096Z" 13194139535137 true] [17592186227527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333180 13194139535137 true] [17592186227527 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333181 13194139535137 true] [17592186227527 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333180 13194139535137 true] [17592186228783 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1,$ the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n\n- $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$" 13194139535137 true] [17592186228783 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = y^3 - x\\,y, \\qquad y(1)=1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x = 1$, the new value $y_\\text{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_\\text{new})$ used in the first step?\n\n1. $g(y_\\text{new}) = (y_\\text{new})^3 - 3y_\\text{new} - 1$\n2. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + 1$\n3. $g(y_\\text{new}) = 3y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 - 1$\n4. $g(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new}$" 13194139535137 false] [17592186228783 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\n&= (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + (y_{new})^{3} - x_{new}y_{new} \\\\\ny_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + x_{new}y_{new} - y_(⏟)_(g(y_{new})) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} + 2y_{new} - 1 \\\\\n&= 3y_{new} - (y_{new})^{3} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535137 false] [17592186228783 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 1$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = y_\\text{new} - (y_\\text{new})^3 + x_\\text{new}y_\\text{new} - y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1 + 1 = 2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1 \\\\  & =3{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535137 true] [17592186228784 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 2xy + y^3, \\qquad y(1.5)=-0.3\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535137 true] [17592186228784 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y' = 2xy + y^3, \\qquad y(1.5)=-0.3$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2)$. Round your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535137 false] [17592186228784 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0)\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\ny'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})\n$$\n\nevaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2$, we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΔy &= y_{new}^{′} \\cdot Δx \\\\\n&= (2x_{new}y_{new} + (y_{new})^{3}) \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into\n\n$$\ny_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y\n$$\n\nand move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{new} &= y + Δy \\\\\ny_{new} &= y + x_{new}y_{new} + 0.5(y_{new})^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y = 0\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 0.5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\nx_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2\n$$\n\nThe old $y$ value is $y = -0.3$. Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ng(y_{new}) &= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + (2 - 1)y_{new} - 0.3 \\\\\n&= 0.5(y_{new})^{3} + y_{new} - 0.3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-0.3$ to solve\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0\n$$\n\nWe'll use Newton's method.\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 1.5(y_\\text{new})^2 + 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{0.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^3 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 0.3}{1.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with\n\n$$\ny(1.5) = -0.3\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y_{new})_{1} &= -0.3 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}\\approx 0.240529 \\\\ (y_{new})_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\\approx 0.288849 \\\\\n&= (y_{new})_{4} \\mid \\approx 0.288050\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(2) \\approx 0.29\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139535137 false] [17592186228784 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\qquad y_0 = y(x_0),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta y = y'_\\text{new} \\cdot \\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwith $y'_\\text{new} = f(x_\\text{new},y_\\text{new})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $\\Delta y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & ={x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into $y_\\text{new} = y + \\Delta y$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g(y_\\text{new}){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $\\Delta x = 0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng(y_\\text{new}) = 0.5(y_\\text{new})^3 + (x_\\text{new}-1)y_\\text{new} + y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is $x_\\text{new} = x + \\Delta x = 1.5 + 0.5 = 2.$ The old $y$ value is $y = -0.3.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+(2-1){y}_{\\text{new}}-0.3 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-0.3$ to solve $g(y_\\text{new}) = 0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{g(y_\\text{new})}{g'(y_\\text{new})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\ng'(y_\\text{new}) = 1.5(y_\\text{new})^2 + 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n(y_\\text{new})_{n+1} = (y_\\text{new})_n - \\dfrac{0.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^3 + (y_\\text{new})_n - 0.3}{1.5((y_\\text{new})_n)^2 + 1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1.5) = -0.3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-0.3 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.240529 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.288849 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.288050\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2) \\approx 0.29,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535137 true] [17592186229106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333182 13194139535137 true] [17592186229106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333189 13194139535137 true] [17592186229106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333196 13194139535137 true] [17592186229106 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333203 13194139535137 true] [17592186229107 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333210 13194139535137 true] [17592186229107 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333214 13194139535137 true] [17592186229107 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333217 13194139535137 true] [17592186229107 1020 #edb/ref 17592186333220 13194139535137 true] [17592186229483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186227521 13194139535137 false] [17592186229483 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333174 13194139535137 true] [17592186229483 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535137 true] [17592186229483 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-x,\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" 13194139535137 true] [17592186229483 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$y^{′} = y^{3} - x, y(1) = 1$\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx = 1$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_{new})$ used in the first step?" 13194139535137 false] [17592186229483 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-y+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-1+2 \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535137 true] [17592186229484 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535137 true] [17592186229484 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nConsider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x^{2} - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535137 false] [17592186229484 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535137 true] [17592186229484 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-(1)^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2