}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.25(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.125}{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.916667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.915476 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.915476\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.5)≈0.92,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535137 true] [17592186333215 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333216 13194139535137 true] [17592186333215 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535137 true] [17592186333215 1041 "field-1" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333215 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333216 13194139535137 true] [17592186333215 1110 #uuid "7e7b9149-4b67-5e4a-b577-ed45ac683e65" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333216 1034 "0.92" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333216 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535137 true] [17592186333216 1111 #uuid "2936ca28-5262-5d62-a6af-0ff25413143e" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333217 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333218 13194139535137 true] [17592186333217 1089 "q-333282" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535137 true] [17592186333217 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333217 1113 #uuid "48eba903-10e9-5e4d-bd8f-9f12d76f76d4" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333217 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-1.2(0.4) \\\\  & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.8({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.4(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.48}{0.8(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.044444 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.044014 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.044014\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈1.04,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535137 true] [17592186333218 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333219 13194139535137 true] [17592186333218 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535137 true] [17592186333218 1041 "field-1" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333218 1104 #edb/ref 17592186333219 13194139535137 true] [17592186333218 1110 #uuid "72c06340-7737-55a2-8f39-11fac3e736eb" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333219 1034 "1.04" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333219 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535137 true] [17592186333219 1111 #uuid "cf65f1c9-4290-588a-aad4-f1be617c9744" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333220 1017 #edb/ref 17592186333221 13194139535137 true] [17592186333220 1089 "q-333286" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333220 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535137 true] [17592186333220 1100 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0.4)=1.2\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.9).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.9)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333220 1113 #uuid "443b0f9b-97de-5973-ba1f-c36595b2443c" 13194139535137 true] [17592186333220 1145 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.9,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.9-0.4=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.4+0.5=0.9.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.2-(0.9) \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1.2$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2.1}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.4)=1.2,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.281818 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.280351 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.280351\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.9)≈1.28,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139535137 true] [17592186333221 1000 #edb/ref 17592186333222 13194139535137 true] [17592186333221 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535137 true] [17592186333221 1041 "field-1" 1319413